    "search": "# Initial Response Yeah, that's a fair point. Honestly, I'm not even sure yet if what we do would be a fit for you… it just depends on what you're doing right now and if you're open to looking at something that could potentially reduce your time spent on infosec compliance by 90%. Would you be open to a a quick conversation to see if it’s worth looking into? 1. Manual (Internal or via Consultants) # Discovery Questions - What made you decide to keep it in-house instead of using a platform? - How are you tracking your evidence and control testing today? - Does it take a lot of manual follow-up across teams? # Pain to Surface - Heavy manual work - Time spent chasing updates - No clear visibility # Scytale Talk Track Yeah, that's what I hear a lot. Teams start out managing it manually, but it ends up taking more time than expected. Scytale just centralizes everything, controls, evidence, and tasks so updates happen automatically and everyone can see what's done and what's pending. Have you ever looked into something like this? 2. Using a Platform (No Consultants) # Discovery Questions - How's your experience been with your platform so far? - Do you feel it gives enough guidance on what's actually needed for the audit? - What parts still feel like a lift for your team? # Pain to Surface - Lack of clarity or guidance - Gaps between automation and readiness - Still relying on internal effort # Scytale Talk Track That makes sense. A lot of platforms automate the basics, but don't really explain what's relevant for your audit. Scytale's different because it's built by auditors, not just devs. You get the automation plus an in-house compliance expert who walks you through what really matters. How long have you used your current platform for? 3. Using Both Platform + Consultants # Discovery Questions - How's that setup been working for you? Do they sync well? - Was the plan always to use both, or did the consultant come in later? # Pain to Surface - Paying for overlap - Disconnected tools and people - No single source of truth # Scytale Talk Track Yeah, that combo works for some, but I've heard it can get messy fast. Scytale brings both pieces together, you get the automation and the advisory under one roof, so you're not bouncing between tools and consultants. It just keeps everything tighter and easier to manage. Does your current solution not come with any advisory? 4. Using Consultants Only (No Platform) # Discovery Questions - How's it been working with your consultants, do they manage most of it, or do you still handle evidence and tracking? - Do you have visibility into where things stand between audits? - How are you tracking vendor risk or access reviews right now? # Pain to Surface - Manual coordination - Limited visibility - Dependency on external help # Scytale Talk Track Makes sense consultants help, but you still end up doing a lot of manual chasing. Scytale combines that same guidance with automation. You still have your compliance expert, but everything's tracked in one place and updated in real time, so you're not waiting on calls or reports. Ever looked at implementing something like this before?"
