    "search": "Validate then Challenge That's actually pretty impressive, not many companies have the resources to build that internally. Can I ask, how long did it take to build and who's maintaining it? Respect that, building your own shows you take it seriously. The question I'd ask though is, is compliance tooling really the best thing for your engineering team to be spending time on? Dig into the Gaps When you say your own platform, does it cover the full cycle? Evidence collection, policy management, audit communication, ongoing monitoring? Or are there parts that are still manual? Has it been tested in a real audit yet? Because a lot of companies find the gaps in their internal build when an auditor starts asking questions it wasn't designed to answer. The Maintenance Angle Building it is one thing, keeping it current is where most internal platforms start to struggle. Frameworks update, controls change, new integrations are needed. Who owns that ongoing work? The challenge with internal builds is they're usually built for where the company is at right now, as you scale, the compliance requirements scale too. Is your platform built to grow with that? The Opportunity Cost Angle How many engineering hours have gone into it so far, and how many are still going in to maintain it? Because that's usually where companies start to question whether it makes sense to keep running it internally. I totally get it - but if you added up the engineering time, the maintenance, the updates every time a framework changes - is it actually cheaper than an external platform? Most companies find it's not when they do the math. The Expert Support Angle The one thing an internal platform can't give you is an in-house compliance expert who's done hundreds of audits. Is there someone internally who owns the advisory side, or is it purely the tooling? A platform is one part of it, but what happens when you hit a grey area in your audit scope, or a control that doesn't map cleanly? Is there an expert behind your internal build or is that on your team to figure out? The Softer Long-Term Play I'm not going to try and tell you to throw away something you've built, that's not a conversation worth having today. But would it be worth a 20-minute call just to see where the gaps might be? If your platform covers everything we do, you'll know for certain. If it doesn't, at least you know what to go build."
